    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Multiple sclerosis diagnosed elsewhere, by her account, with records of the original evaluation not available: slowly worsening walking over the past few years, leg stiffness, fatigue, urinary urgency and heat sensitivity; uses a cane; course to be classified after MRI of the brain and spine</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>G35.D</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting picture at intake, superseded by the classified diagnosis at the consultation; the cane and a diagnosis around the time she left Congress are from public accounts.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Active primary progressive multiple sclerosis: gradual worsening of walking over more than a year without relapses, with brain lesions in periventricular, juxtacortical and infratentorial regions, three spinal cord lesions, and one gadolinium-enhancing lesion; fulfils the 1971 McDonald criteria for primary progressive disease; EDSS 6.0; synthetic modern detail linked to her publicly reported multiple sclerosis</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>G35.B1</td><td>S — Synthetic course, MRI findings and disability score; the disease itself and her use of a cane are from public accounts.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Spastic gait from multiple sclerosis, walking with a cane, with two near-falls and no falls in the past year; at risk of falls</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R26.89</td><td>S — Synthetic modern coding anchored to her documented cane.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-4">
    <td>Neurogenic bladder from multiple sclerosis: urgency and getting up twice at night, with a low post-void residual</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>N31.9</td><td>S — Synthetic; common in multiple sclerosis. No public source describes her bladder.</td>
